00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.600
And from there, she
writes her SQL query.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:25.560
She's super productive.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:26.820
She gets her answers.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:27.840
She exports the data.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.340
She sends CSV files
to people and so on.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.940
But if you wanted to go from
BigQuery to Data Studio, how

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.470
exactly do you do that?

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.000
Well, it can be
as simple as this.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.450
I just go from a BigQuery
table and I go to export.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:41.508
And I say explore
in Data Studio.

00:05:41.508 --> 00:05:43.800
And it's going to create the
connection for you, set it

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:44.610
all up.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.610
And in a moment here,
we'll see a live connection

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.780
to the BigQuery table and some
results that are coming back.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.120
And when you do this, and
you actually save your work,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.860
one of the artifacts
that gets created

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:57.200
is called a data source.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:58.950
It's what Data Studio
calls a data source.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.700
And it is the proxy for your
data, for your BigQuery table.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.380
It's a secure connection to it.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.540
It knows how to
authenticate to it.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.570
You can give all of your fields
friendly human readable names.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.820
You can add additional
calculated fields.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.730
And now if you share this with
your users instead of the CSV

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.980
file or a spreadsheet,
they're one click away

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.540
from creating a
report on top of this

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.490
without worrying about
prepping the data,

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.920
or connecting to the data,
or just maybe visually

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:24.160
exploring the data.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.185
And that's what I'm
going to focus on today.

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:27.810
You know Data Studio
as this great tool

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.480
to be able to design these
really beautiful reports.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.180
But right now, I don't even
know what report I want.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.110
I'm new to this data.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.940
I want to familiarize
myself with it

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.250
I want to see what's in it, what
interesting metrics and trends.

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:41.490
What are some different ways
of visualizing the data?

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.170
And so I want just
a really productive

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:45.880
rapid visual
exploration experience.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.880
And so that's Data
Studio Explorer.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.380
It's a different
part of Data Studio

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.640
that many people
haven't played with yet.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.970
But it's getting better
and better with every month

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.120
that we invest into it.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.130
And I want to show you.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.770
So one of the things
I might do first

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.750
is I might just have
a metadata question,

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.760
like, how large
is this data set?

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.530
How large is this table?

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.620
This scorecard will aggregate
a metric into a single number.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.990
And so it automatically
created a record count metric

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:11.760
for me, which is
the number of rows.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.843
And so this is going to
aggregate the total number

00:07:13.843 --> 00:07:15.310
of rows in the table.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:16.810
And just like that,
I can see I have

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:20.128
116 million rows worth of
data in this BigQuery table.

00:07:20.128 --> 00:07:21.670
So all of the
performance that you're

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:24.070
going to see as I'm clicking
around, drilling, slicing,

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:27.910
filtering is doing live
queries over 116 million rows

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:28.690
worth of data.

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.490
And it's still going to be fast.

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:32.440
So each row represents a flight.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:36.520
So this is 116 million flights
over the last almost 20 years

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:38.640
or so worth of data.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.390
Another analytic
technique that I will use

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:43.560
is looking at the same
data in different ways

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:45.310
through different types
of visualizations,

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:47.842
different types of charts,
or in this case, maybe a map.

00:07:47.842 --> 00:07:49.300
What I want to do
is I want to look

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.722
at which states have
the most flights

00:07:51.722 --> 00:07:53.680
departing from them,
which states have the most

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:55.690
departures.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:58.270
And just like that, it live
queried 116 million rows,

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.730
geocoded the states
based on their name

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:02.080
and plotted them on a map.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:03.880
And they're colored
in color intensity

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:07.240
