00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:23.060
Here, I can do multi select.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:25.503
You'll see the Pacific
Northwest selected in the map.

00:13:25.503 --> 00:13:26.920
And I can drill
down and see these

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:30.140
are the cities with the most
departures in the Pacific

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:31.870
Northwest.

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:34.322
OK, we haven't done any
time series analysis yet.

00:13:34.322 --> 00:13:35.780
So maybe that would
be a good point

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:38.430
to introduce that, so look
at some trends over time.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:39.980
So maybe let me
multi select these.

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:41.938
And I'm just going to
make a little bit of room

00:13:41.938 --> 00:13:45.110
so that I can do some
light rearrangement

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:49.520
and make some space for me to
introduce another chart here.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.680
So you can do some light
layout in Explorer.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:53.790
Let's add a time series chart.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.670
And again, my automatic
record count metric

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:58.310
is just going to show
me the number of flights

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:01.100
over this almost
20-year time period.

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:03.210
And one of the things
we see is that there are

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:04.460
some months with missing data.

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:06.500
So no data is without
its data quality issues.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:08.292
There's also a lot of
noise because there's

00:14:08.292 --> 00:14:09.980
seasonal fluctuation.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:12.543
I'm really just interested
in the long term trend.

00:14:12.543 --> 00:14:13.710
Here, I'm going to drill up.

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:15.752
I didn't even configure
a hierarchy in this case.

00:14:15.752 --> 00:14:19.020
It just knows that this
is a date time data type.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:21.060
And so it added year,
month, and day for me.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:23.270
I can just drill up
without any configuration.

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:24.290
That's month.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.700
And that's year.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:27.620
Automatic drill down.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:30.360
Now, you'll notice that
it plummets in 2018,

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.110
and that doesn't seem
to make sense to me.

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:33.693
Because if I drill
back down to month,

00:14:33.693 --> 00:14:35.940
I don't see it plummeting
at the end of 2018.

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:38.190
The thing is, what's
going on here?

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:42.210
It looks like the data only
goes up until July, August 2018.

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:44.942
So I don't have
full data for 2018.

00:14:44.942 --> 00:14:46.650
Look how easy this is
to filter this out.

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:48.150
I only want to look
at this by year.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:49.920
And I want to drag year up here.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.840
And I'm going to say, OK,
2018 has partial data.

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:54.180
So let me just filter that out.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:56.013
I'm doing the opposite
of what I did before.

00:14:56.013 --> 00:14:57.000
I don't want only 2018.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.820
I want everything but 2018.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:14:59.910
I'll click Apply.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:02.070
And I've filtered out that
year with partial data

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.457
just like that.

00:15:03.457 --> 00:15:05.040
So that's the number
of flights that's

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:06.840
been increasing over the years.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:08.340
It would be interesting to
see what other metrics I

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:08.950
have in here.

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:09.750
So let's see.

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:11.520
I'll go to my metric picture.

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:14.430
And I think we've got some
data on flight delays.

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:16.890
So I've got, yeah,
like departure delay.

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:19.890
Do you think that the average
departure delay has gone down

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.220
or up over the last few years?

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:25.990
How many people
think it's gone down?

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:26.970
That's pretty sad.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:29.370
How many people
think it's gone up?

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:30.750
OK, the vast majority of you.

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:33.480
OK, so let's drag
departure delay.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:35.670
And departure delays
have been going up

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:38.430
for at least the
last 17 years or so.

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.170
They've been getting
gradually worse

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:45.420
from about 6 minutes to 17,
18 minutes in the last year

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:47.170
or couple years ago.

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:48.937
So is that trend true
everywhere, though?

00:15:48.937 --> 00:15:51.270
And again, you'll see the
power of this cross filtering.

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.730
I can explore my data
with minimal to little

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:54.837
to no configuration.

00:15:54.837 --> 00:15:55.920
I noticed Seattle in here.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:56.740
I live in Seattle.

00:15:56.740 --> 00:15:59.600
And so I do the jet commute
from Seattle to-- oh,

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:04.770
some Seattle people, OK,
or person, one person, OK.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.490
So I can look at, does
this trend hold true?

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:11.650
Has the delays been increasing
for Seattle to San Francisco?

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:12.657
I just filter that.

00:16:12.657 --> 00:16:13.740
And it hasn't, thankfully.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.780
So thankfully for me,
I guess that flight

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.060
has been holding steady.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:20.360
What other metrics
do I have in here?

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:22.470
So I've got departure delay.

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:25.410
I can also add arrival delay.

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.217
So look at arrival
delay alongside that.

00:16:28.217 --> 00:16:30.300
And that actually has been
steady, or if anything,

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:32.220
decreasing as well.

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:33.990
Other metrics, I
think I have a metric

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:37.650
in here about the actual
elapsed flight time.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:39.210
Maybe that's also
what I care about.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:40.793
How long is it going
to take me to fly

00:16:40.793 --> 00:16:42.180
from Seattle to San Francisco?

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:44.790
I mean, this I wouldn't
think would change very much,

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:46.567
except it has a lot.

00:16:46.567 --> 00:16:48.150
What's interesting
is this has gone up

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:52.650
a lot between 2004 and 2007, and
then again really skyrocketing

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:54.000
the last couple of years.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.250
If the flight's not
delayed, then why

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:59.610
would the actual time of
the flight be taking longer?

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:01.650
Well, I mean, I
guess it's possible

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:04.089
that they've just scheduled
the flight to take longer.

00:17:04.089 --> 00:17:08.877
So I actually have the scheduled
elapsed time of the flight.

00:17:08.877 --> 00:17:10.460
And sure enough,
those are correlated.

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:11.770
So they've actually
just been scheduling

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.540
my flight from Seattle
to San Francisco

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:14.980
to take longer and longer.

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:18.859
Why would they schedule
a flight to take longer?

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:20.060
Busy airports.

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:21.215
OK, what could that impact?

00:17:21.215 --> 00:17:22.190
MARY PISHNY: Fuel.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:24.030
RICCARDO MUTI: Fuel,
slower to fuel.

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:24.780
MARY PISHNY: Yeah.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:24.950
RICCARDO MUTI: I don't know.

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:25.550
What do you think?

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:27.560
MARY PISHNY: Can you take
