going on there?

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:31.422
RICCARDO MUTI: Yes.

00:17:31.422 --> 00:17:34.250
So maybe the time is spent on
the ground and not in the air.

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:36.320
So I can search for taxi.

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:38.480
The problem is, as you
just pointed out, yeah,

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:41.090
I guess there's taxi out
and there's taxi back in.

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:43.050
I'm actually interested
in the sum of those.

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:43.820
I don't have that.

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:46.220
But I can easily create
that by just creating

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:50.830
a calculated field and saying,
I want taxi out plus taxi in.

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:53.330
And of course, I can do other
calculations as well, but just

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:59.230
on the fly, taxi
in, click Apply.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:00.480
Does that trend look familiar?

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:02.760
A big increase
from 2004 to 2007,

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:04.320
and again, last couple of years.

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:08.280
Taxi time has gone from 19
minutes to almost half an hour.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:10.920
So is that trend holding
across other airports

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:12.070
in the United States?

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:14.940
If I look at Chicago, it was
getting better for a long while

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:18.060
until the last five years or so
that it's been getting worse.

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:21.510
In New York, it's kind of always
been bad, over half an hour.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:23.820
But it looks like
in 2007 and 2008,

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:27.900
it was close to 45 minutes
on the ground in a plane.

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:30.300
So these are just
some of the insights

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.340
that you can get by
visually exploring

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:33.270
