So looking at flights by
carrier, cancellations,

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:02.230
arrival, and departure delays.

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:04.390
And then I also have a
San Francisco deep dive.

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:07.390
And I'm ready to drop
those charts that we just

00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:10.910
created into our report.

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:13.000
So all I did was
save that analysis.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:13.990
I copied those charts.

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:16.030
And then I brought
it in here, so I

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:20.360
can continue to work with this
data and start sharing it out.

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:22.940
So I'm going to apply some
light styling changes.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:24.680
OK, actually looks pretty good.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:26.538
Let me drop this
down a little bit.

00:20:26.538 --> 00:20:28.330
OK, so the first thing
I want to solve here

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:31.290
is that I could share this
out with my viewers as is.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:32.110
Sure.

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:35.000
But I want them to still be
able to interact with this data.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:37.240
So I can add controls
to the page that make

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:39.890
that really easy for our users.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:41.960
So one is origin city name.

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:44.690
So they can quickly
select what origin

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:47.480
of the flights taking off from.

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:49.190
And I'm also going
to add one so they

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:51.530
can filter by carrier name.

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:55.520
So let me-- carrier name.

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:56.900
Perfect.

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:59.360
So what that looks like
here, I can come in

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:03.670
and say, show me all
the United flights.

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:07.207
Do a quick select, and that's
going to update on the fly.

00:21:07.207 --> 00:21:08.040
RICCARDO MUTI: Cool.

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:08.630
MARY PISHNY: Yeah.

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:10.400
RICCARDO MUTI: So one of the
things that I often find,

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:13.250
or that we often see as well is
that you share this report out

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:14.270
with people, and
then they come back

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:16.115
with questions like,
what does this mean?

00:21:16.115 --> 00:21:17.490
Am I interpreting
this correctly?

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:18.532
How do I use this report?

00:21:18.532 --> 00:21:20.780
So do you have any tips
for how to solve that?

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:21.050
MARY PISHNY: Yes.

00:21:21.050 --> 00:21:22.460
That's a common
problem or a challenge

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:24.293
that we hear is, how
do I educate users when

00:21:24.293 --> 00:21:25.860
they're looking at this data?

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:27.360
So what I'm going
to do in this case

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:29.210
is we have something
called URL embed

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:33.920
that makes it easy to embed
other content directly

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:35.510
into your report.

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:38.780
So in this case, we
have flight data.

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:42.060
And it's looking at San
Francisco in particular.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:45.290
So I actually found
a YouTube video.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:49.520
And it gives an overview of
the San Francisco airport

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:50.850
annual report.

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:53.330
And so I'm actually going
to include this directly

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:57.650
into my report.

00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:01.095
And so users, as they're
interacting with the data,

00:22:01.095 --> 00:22:02.720
can come in and learn
a little bit more

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:05.030
about San Francisco
operations, give them

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:08.400
some context for as they're
consuming this data.

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.280
And then lastly,
it's pretty common

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:11.600
that as you're going
through some analysis,

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:12.433
you're taking notes.

00:22:12.433 --> 00:22:15.140
You're finding some call
outs about the schema.

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:18.812
I've put those all in just a
quick doc with some information

00:22:18.812 --> 00:22:21.020
about the data set, some
information about the schema

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:22.710
itself, some delay codes.

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:24.890
And I'm going to take
that same approach here.

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:27.280
And instead of having to
send this over in an email,

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:30.870
I'm going to actually just embed
that directly into the report.

00:22:30.870 --> 00:22:34.080
So I can now interact
with both the video--

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:36.410
I won't play it for us;
it's a little dated--

00:22:36.410 --> 00:22:39.470
and this document that
has some more context

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:41.390
on my analysis all right here.

00:22:41.390 --> 00:22:44.240
So pretty powerful.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:47.200
So now that I have this
interactive viewing experience,

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:50.080
I've tailored it so they can
consume the data right away.

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:52.760
And now I'm ready to actually
start distributing that data.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:55.880
So we have a couple different
options based on the audience.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:58.540
Number one is I can come in here
and just download this report.

00:22:58.540 --> 00:23:00.832
We know that a lot of people
still operate in PDF mode,

00:23:00.832 --> 00:23:04.730
so we can quickly get a
PDF to download with them.

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:06.980
I can also just share the
link with them directly,

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:09.976
so they can quickly go
and consume this online.

00:23:09.976 --> 00:23:11.840
RICCARDO MUTI: The
download PDF feature's

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:12.973
definitely really useful.

00:23:12.973 --> 00:23:14.390
I think one thing
we find often is

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:16.460
people are asking me to
email them the updated

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:17.840
report every week.

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:19.882
So is there a way that we
can just automate that?

00:23:19.882 --> 00:23:21.423
MARY PISHNY: You
set me up, Riccardo.

00:23:21.423 --> 00:23:22.160
This is perfect.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:25.520
So we have email
delivery, which delivers

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:27.770
the report in your inbox.

00:23:27.770 --> 00:23:29.090
It's a really rich experience.

00:23:29.090 --> 00:23:31.550
I actually want to give you
guys all a little homework

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:33.380
and go try this out today.

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:35.380
It puts the report
directly into your inbox.

00:23:35.380 --> 00:23:38.370
So in a lot of cases, you can
consume that data right there.

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:39.290
Sometimes, you might
want to click through

00:23:39.290 --> 00:23:40.460
to learn a little bit more.

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:41.660
It's also available in PDF.

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:43.820
So you can make sure that
if you want your team

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:45.833
to be seeing this every
Monday or every day

00:23:45.833 --> 00:23:47.750
you're looking at this
data, you can make sure

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:50.600
that this is in front of them
in a place where we spend

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:53.760
a lot of our time these days.

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:55.520
So we've gone
through the analysis

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:58.340
with Riccardo, as well as
actually taking that report,

00:23:58.340 --> 00:24:00.860
making it into something
that's consumable, but still

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:03.240
interactive and
distributing it out.

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:05.370
We're going to jump
back to Ruth's story.

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:07.340
And with that, we're
to bring Minhaz up

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:10.580
on the stage, who's going to
show us what's next for Ruth.

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:14.563
All right.

00:24:14.563 --> 00:24:16.230
MINHAZ KAZI: All
right, thank you, Mary.

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:16.730
Hi.

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:17.530
I'm Minhaz Kazi.

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:19.690
I'm one of the developer
advocates for Data Studio.

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:21.940
And we're going to take you
through the rest of Ruth's

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:22.870
story.

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:25.370
So Ruth has been doing
very well at her job.

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:27.550
She has set up this whole
entire data pipeline

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:29.530
based on BigQuery BI Engine.

00:24:29.530 --> 00:24:32.650
She has set up all
the dashboards, set up

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:33.535
the PDF exports.

00:24:33.535 --> 00:24:34.660
People are getting reports.

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:37.120
People are running analyses.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:39.730
Everything has been going
really well for Ruth

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:43.710
until something really
terrible happens.

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:48.380
Ruth gets promoted
to be a manager.

00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:50.630
So now Ruth has a
different set of problems.

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:52.790
Because she had
solved all that issue

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.800
where people were
emailing her about,

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:56.220
can you write this SQL for me?

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:59.510
Or can you debug
this SQL code for me?

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:03.590
But it went from being that
to people setting up meetings

00:25:03.590 --> 00:25:06.560
with her and people setting
up emails saying that, hey,

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:07.580
my dashboard is broken.

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:08.720
Can you fix it?

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:10.395
My dashboard is
running very slow.

00:25:10.395 --> 00:25:11.270
Can you fix that one?

00:25:11.270 --> 00:25:13.070
I've been getting
this code error issue.

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:14.333
What is this about?

00:25:14.333 --> 00:25:16.250
So she starts getting
all of these complaints.

00:25:16.250 --> 00:25:17.960
And she starts
taking note of them,

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:20.130
and she wants to solve
this with her team.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:22.880
So the major problem she
runs across is again,

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:25.890
can we make all the
dashboards fast?

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:28.370
Now, in Ruth's department,
they were using the BI Engine

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:30.000
to make things faster.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.750
However, at this point,
all the other departments

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:35.510
in her organization are not
using the same BI Engine

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:36.102
approach.

00:25:36.102 --> 00:25:38.060
For example, marketing
has been pulling in data

00:25:38.060 --> 00:25:41.480
from Google
Analytics, Google Ads.

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:44.100
Finals has been putting
their data in CSV files

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:45.400
and in Sheets.

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:48.440
HR has set up their own My
SQL instance in the basement

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:49.280
in a server.

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:51.840
So all of those reports
need to be faster.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:54.450
So what is the solution
that Ruth came up with?

00:25:54.450 --> 00:25:57.467
Well, you obviously can migrate
to the BigQuery BI Engine.

00:25:57.467 --> 00:25:59.300
You can take your data,
put it in BI Engine,

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:01.110
and pull the reports from there.

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:03.470
And if you think about the
spectrum where you have,

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:06.440
I want really fast
dashboards, and I want my data

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:08.930
to be updated regularly,
BI Engine actually

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:10.680
takes care of both of these.

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:13.280
However, if that's
not an option for you,

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:15.320
then you can go the
extract data way,

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:17.810
which will let you make
really fast dashboards.

00:26:17.810 --> 00:26:22.033
So let's take a look
at how that works.

00:26:22.033 --> 00:26:23.450
Over here, I have
a dashboard that

00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:25.970
is pulling in live Twitter
data from the Twitter

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:29.120
API for the Google
Data Studio hashtag.

00:26:29.120 --> 00:26:31.080
And this is a
Twitter data source.

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:33.602
If I view this, and
then I refresh it,

00:26:33.602 --> 00:26:35.060
you'll see that it
takes some time.

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:36.435
It will reach into
the connector.

00:26:36.435 --> 00:26:37.970
It will pull the API.

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:39.360
It will format the data.

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:43.700
So it takes some time to update
and refresh the dashboard.

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:46.760
If we want to make this
faster, what we could do

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:49.580
is go back in, select
the data source,

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:52.362
and use the extract
feature on it.

00:26:52.362 --> 00:26:54.320
And to use the extract
feature, what we will do

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:58.850
is select extract data
as a new connector.

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:02.270
And so this is a new
connector, extract data.

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:04.730
Over on the left side, I will
have all the data sources

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:06.030
that I've created previously.

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:09.110
So I'll use the
Twitter data source.

00:27:09.110 --> 00:27:11.070
I'll add that to the report.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:14.120
I'll pick the fields
that are relevant for me.

00:27:14.120 --> 00:27:21.310
So pick screen name, Twitter
text, date, retweet count.

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:24.670
And I'll pull back the
date as a date range.

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:28.240
As I click this Save and Extract
button, what Data Studio does

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:31.330
is reaches into the connector,
pulls in all the data,

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:34.750
and saves it on GCS
as a small bucket,

00:27:34.750 --> 00:27:36.820
where all this data
is materialized

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:38.410
as a static source.

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:41.310
So I'll add this
back to the report.

00:27:41.310 --> 00:27:42.990
And as I look at
the report, I see

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:45.480
there's this new untitled
data source that the one

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:47.700
that I just treated as a
materialized data source.

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:50.340
And if I view this,
and I click on Refresh,

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:51.690
you can see it's very fast.

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:53.315
So you can take
this data source,

00:27:53.315 --> 00:27:55.440
and then you can create
new charts, tables with it,

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:58.990
and it'll remain very fast.

00:27:58.990 --> 00:28:01.030
So that is one option.

00:28:01.030 --> 00:28:03.850
The limitations of extract
data is that the data set

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:05.380
is limited to 100 megabytes.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:07.270
And we're working
on a feature which

00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:09.798
will let you automatically
schedule updates

00:28:09.798 --> 00:28:10.840
through the extract data.

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:12.048
So you can set up scheduling.

00:28:12.048 --> 00:28:13.810
And the data extract
would automatically

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:15.910
get updated at a
certain interval.

00:28:15.910 --> 00:28:18.880
But what if you want to work on
the other end of the spectrum

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:22.450
where you want really
fresh data for your users?

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:25.493
As a dashboard owner, you can
always refresh the dashboard.

00:28:25.493 --> 00:28:27.910
But if you want the dashboard
to be updated for your users

00:28:27.910 --> 00:28:31.240
as well, then you need
a different approach.

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:33.340
And then Ruth's
solution was basically

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:36.290
to use the new feature
of data freshness.

00:28:36.290 --> 00:28:39.100
This is available both
for BigQuery and Sheets,

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:42.130
where you can go into the
options for a data source

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:45.550
and set the interval by
which the data source should

00:28:45.550 --> 00:28:46.330
be updated.

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:49.370
For Sheets, it'll be between
15 minutes to 12 hours.

00:28:49.370 --> 00:28:53.540
And for BigQuery, it'll be
between one hour to 12 hours.

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:55.960
So this was Ruth's
first week as a manager.

00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:57.670
The first week goes by.

00:28:57.670 --> 00:28:59.260
She goes home,
spends the weekend.

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:01.780
She comes back on
Monday, opens her email,

00:29:01.780 --> 00:29:04.240
and this is the first
email she choose.

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:07.280
It's an email from the
compliance department.

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:10.140
All of these reports you are
making and sharing with people,

00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:12.620
what are you doing
about data governance?

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:14.180
Are you making
sure that everyone

00:29:14.180 --> 00:29:17.570
is viewing exactly the data
that they have access to?

00:29:17.570 --> 00:29:19.310
We know that we
have a lot of data

00:29:19.310 --> 00:29:21.382
that can be shared
across organizations,

00:29:21.382 --> 00:29:23.090
across the entire
organization, but there

00:29:23.090 --> 00:29:24.882
are other data sets
that can be shared only

00:29:24.882 --> 00:29:26.010
for specific people.

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:28.160
So people shouldn't be
viewing all the reports.

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:32.150
They should be able to view only
the stuff that they have access

00:29:32.150 --> 00:29:34.100
to.

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:36.560
For this, she already
had an existing solution,

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:39.440
which was enforcing
viewer's credentials.

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:41.510
For each data source
in Data Studio,

00:29:41.510 --> 00:29:43.100
you can go into
the data set option

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:46.280
and set the data
source access level

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:47.810
to either owners or viewers.

00:29:47.810 --> 00:29:49.940
Owners lets you
view the data source

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:53.420
based on whoever created
the dashboard or the report.

00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:56.240
But viewers ensures that if
you're viewing the report,

00:29:56.240 --> 00:29:59.070
your credentials would get
passed into the data source.

00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:01.220
And if you don't have
access to the data source,

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:02.940
you cannot view the data.

00:30:02.940 --> 00:30:05.420
For example, let's say there
is a worksheet with all the HR

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:07.340
information about employees.

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:10.580
HR department has access to
it, but the sales department

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:11.460
does not.

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:14.540
So if you create a dashboard
from that data source

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:16.910
and then shared that
with other people,

00:30:16.910 --> 00:30:18.920
the HR employees will
see what they're supposed

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:20.420
to see on the left side.

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:23.960
But if someone has access to
the dashboard who is not an HR

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:25.820
employee, not within
that organization,

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:27.860
they'll still be able
to open the dashboard,

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:30.500
but they'll just see that you
don't have access to this data

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:34.350
because that sheet was
not shared with you.

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:38.788
So this is done at a complete
entire data set level.

00:30:38.788 --> 00:30:39.830
MARY PISHNY: Hey, Minhaz.

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:41.390
Can you talk us
through what we can

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:45.008
do if we need the security to
be administered on a row basis?

00:30:45.008 --> 00:30:46.550
MINHAZ KAZI: That
is a good question.

00:30:46.550 --> 00:30:49.550
Because what we're showing
right now is at an entire data

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:50.540
set basis.

00:30:50.540 --> 00:30:52.760
But oftentimes,
organizations would

00:30:52.760 --> 00:30:54.620
require role level access.

00:30:54.620 --> 00:30:57.110
For example, in Ruth's team,
there are two analysts.

00:30:57.110 --> 00:31:00.500
One of the analysts
works on the West Coast.

00:31:00.500 --> 00:31:03.530
They focus mostly on
California and Oregon.

00:31:03.530 --> 00:31:09.260
And another analyst focuses
on the DC metro area, the DC,

00:31:09.260 --> 00:31:10.710
Virginia, and Maryland.

00:31:10.710 --> 00:31:13.820
So the data she
has on flights, she

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:15.320
would like to
separate out this data

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:20.300
so that people can only see
what they have access to.

00:31:20.300 --> 00:31:24.260
And to do that, they will
want to implement role level

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:25.040
security.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:26.360
Ruth was trying to find ways.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:27.770
What can she do?

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:29.750
So there was this
analyst in her team

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:32.450
who used to work as a
developer a long time ago.

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:34.730
They have some familiarity
with JavaScript,

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:37.340
but they're not a
full blown developer.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:38.850
They're not a coder.

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:41.330
So they looked into the Data
Studio's developer website,

00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:43.310
and they realized that
they can get this done

00:31:43.310 --> 00:31:45.633
using community connectors.

00:31:45.633 --> 00:31:47.300
They can implement a
community connector

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:50.060
and enforce viewer's
credentials with it.

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:53.510
The way it would work
is basically over here,

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:56.460
let's say, we have a report.

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:59.810
This is the report view for
the analyst who is viewing

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:01.330
the data for West Coast.

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:04.400
So they can only see
California and Oregon data.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:07.010
And here are all the cities
for California and Oregon.

00:32:07.010 --> 00:32:10.940
I'll take the exact same
report, copy the URL,

00:32:10.940 --> 00:32:13.760
and I'll open it up for
the other analyst who has

00:32:13.760 --> 00:32:16.370
access to the East Coast data.

00:32:16.370 --> 00:32:18.860
So I'll paste in the
exact same report URL.

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:20.570
I won't change anything else.

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:22.240
And as I access this
report, you will

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:24.710
see that this person will
not see the West Coast data.

00:32:24.710 --> 00:32:27.100
They'll see only
the East Coast data.

00:32:27.100 --> 00:32:28.910
So you have Virginia
and Maryland.

00:32:28.910 --> 00:32:31.040
So these are the exact
two same reports.

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:34.640
But over here, based on who
is accessing the report,

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:37.230
you're filtering the data
for the same data source.

00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:38.300
It's the same table.

00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:40.400
Only the rows are
getting filtered based

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:41.860
on who is viewing the report.

00:32:44.643 --> 00:32:47.310
And to do that, all they have to
do, all the analysts have to do

00:32:47.310 --> 00:32:51.840
is go into the Data Studio's
developer's website, figure out

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:53.930
how this community
connector works.

00:32:53.930 --> 00:32:55.610
We have made it much easier.

00:32:55.610 --> 00:32:58.440
Previously, you'd have to use
the BigQuery connector using

00:32:58.440 --> 00:32:59.273
the REST API.

00:32:59.273 --> 00:33:00.690
But we've introduced
a new feature

00:33:00.690 --> 00:33:04.650
called advanced services that
lets you pass configuration

00:33:04.650 --> 00:33:06.635
directly from Data
Studio into BigQuery.

00:33:06.635 --> 00:33:08.010
So if you're
writing a connector,

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:10.530
the only thing you need to
pass is just the configuration

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:12.095
that I want data
from this table.

00:33:12.095 --> 00:33:13.470
This is how you
should filter it.

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:15.210
And these are the
access token, which

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:17.088
makes the code very small.

00:33:17.088 --> 00:33:19.630
The connector that I showed you,
the dashboard, the connector

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:21.300
code for that is this.

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:22.230
This is it.

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:24.960
This is only like probably
100 lines of code.

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:27.540
And most of it is just
configuration data.

00:33:27.540 --> 00:33:29.190
There is no business
logic involved.

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:32.190
So anyone with minimal level
of understanding of JavaScript

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:34.440
should be able to implement
this in a very short time.

00:33:37.190 --> 00:33:39.380
So that gets implemented.

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:41.090
A lot of people in
Ruth's organization

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:42.520
are now using Data Studio.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:44.240
They are creating reports.

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:46.250
A new problem comes up.

00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:49.318
It takes a long time for
people to get ramped up.

00:33:49.318 --> 00:33:51.110
If they have never used
Data Studio before,

00:33:51.110 --> 00:33:53.990
if they have never used BigQuery
before, once they start,

00:33:53.990 --> 00:33:55.630
it takes them some
time to learn it.

00:33:55.630 --> 00:33:59.210
So to go from just data
to a completed dashboard,

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:02.050
it takes maybe days or hours.

00:34:02.050 --> 00:34:04.940
So a director comes by
Ruth's desk and asks, hey,

00:34:04.940 --> 00:34:08.409
can we make this whole
approach much faster?

00:34:08.409 --> 00:34:10.480
Can we maybe
implement templates so

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:13.150
that a new user can come
in, start with a template,

00:34:13.150 --> 00:34:16.480
and they can get started
just like that, pretty fast?

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:19.239
And since a lot of users
will be doing this,

00:34:19.239 --> 00:34:21.445
we also need to keep
this under cost.

00:34:21.445 --> 00:34:24.070
We have to have cost control and
make sure the performance does

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:28.130
not get bogged down, even though
a lot of people are using it.

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:30.980
So Ruth remembers that last
year at the Next video,

00:34:30.980 --> 00:34:35.600
she saw a similar solution,
which was the Chrome UX report.

00:34:35.600 --> 00:34:40.797
It was a connector
created with Data Studio.

00:34:40.797 --> 00:34:42.380
This is what the
connector looks like.

00:34:42.380 --> 00:34:45.110
It's you basically go
in, this connector has

00:34:45.110 --> 00:34:47.060
about more than a
terabyte of data,

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:48.800
about 5 million different URLs.

00:34:48.800 --> 00:34:50.179
You just put in a URL.

00:34:50.179 --> 00:34:53.350
And this is exactly the workflow
a normal user would complete.

00:34:53.350 --> 00:34:54.710
They go to this URL.

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:57.110
They put in their
configuration information.

00:34:57.110 --> 00:34:59.170
I'll just type in Google.com.

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:01.220
Click Connect.

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:05.000
And this pulls in the data
from BigQuery, configures it.

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:07.190
And then as I click
Create Report,

00:35:07.190 --> 00:35:12.260
this lands me into a templated
report with my configured data.

00:35:12.260 --> 00:35:16.460
So a regular user that had
previously no experience

00:35:16.460 --> 00:35:18.680
with BigQuery or Data
Studio whatsoever

00:35:18.680 --> 00:35:22.100
can go from just nothing to
their configured template

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:25.130
that they can edit and share
with other people in just four

00:35:25.130 --> 00:35:26.347
clicks.

00:35:26.347 --> 00:35:28.430
This is something we already
showed you last year.

00:35:28.430 --> 00:35:31.110
But we have made more
improvements from this.

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:33.050
So this is a report
for Google.com.

00:35:33.050 --> 00:35:35.020
If we want to change
the configuration,

00:35:35.020 --> 00:35:37.520
if you want to see this for
a different website, what

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:39.950
we would have to do is
go back to the connector,

00:35:39.950 --> 00:35:42.680
put in a new configuration,
go through the flow again,

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:44.920
and that would create
us a new report.

00:35:44.920 --> 00:35:49.910
So you would get new reports for
every URL, for every website.

00:35:49.910 --> 00:35:52.850
We tried to make this
simpler, so what we did

00:35:52.850 --> 00:35:54.440
was a scalable report.

00:35:54.440 --> 00:35:56.130
This is the exact same report.

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:58.400
But when you enable
the URL parameters,

00:35:58.400 --> 00:36:01.640
you can just go in and
change the configuration

00:36:01.640 --> 00:36:02.930
through the URL.

00:36:02.930 --> 00:36:05.060
So this is showing
data for Wikipedia.

00:36:05.060 --> 00:36:07.487
I can just go in, in
the URL parameters,

00:36:07.487 --> 00:36:09.070
I just change this
for Stack Overflow.

00:36:12.720 --> 00:36:15.540
It's still the same
report, but it will now

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:17.940
change the configuration,
call the connector again,

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.460
fetch the new data,
cache the new data,

00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:22.110
and show you the
information for Stack

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:24.370
Overflow in the same dashboard.

00:36:24.370 --> 00:36:29.220
So the viewer's credentials
paired with this URL parameter

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:32.250
lets you do massive
scalable reporting.

00:36:32.250 --> 00:36:34.350
You can just create one
report and roll it out

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:35.963
to thousands of users.

00:36:35.963 --> 00:36:37.380
They will only see
the information

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:38.440
that they have access to.

00:36:38.440 --> 00:36:40.650
You can also take a
report and then pass

00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:42.300
configurations in the URL.

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:44.240
And the user will end
up seeing different data

00:36:44.240 --> 00:36:46.320
based on what configuration
you are passing.

00:36:51.330 --> 00:36:55.010
So this is the architecture
of the connector

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:56.600
that we showed you last year.

00:36:56.600 --> 00:36:57.740
So that gets solved.

00:36:57.740 --> 00:37:01.037
People are now using all of
their internal data in the FAA.

00:37:01.037 --> 00:37:02.120
They're creating analyses.

00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:03.770
They're creating dashboards.

00:37:03.770 --> 00:37:06.682
An analyst in Ruth's
team comes up and says,

00:37:06.682 --> 00:37:07.890
I found something externally.

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:09.440
There is an external
data set that we

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:11.070
think would be really helpful.

00:37:11.070 --> 00:37:12.770
It is not within
our organization.

00:37:12.770 --> 00:37:14.150
It is not on BigQuery.

00:37:14.150 --> 00:37:15.950
It is not in our database.

00:37:15.950 --> 00:37:20.540
But we'd like to bring that in
and use that with Data Studio.

00:37:20.540 --> 00:37:23.000
And this data set was on Kaggle.

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:26.602
So how could we bring in
Kaggle data into Data Studio?

00:37:26.602 --> 00:37:28.310
There was a solution
available because we

00:37:28.310 --> 00:37:32.210
launched the integration
between Kaggle and Data Studio.

00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:35.550
Let's see an example for that.

00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:38.980
Maybe I'll go onto
the Kaggle website.

00:37:38.980 --> 00:37:43.370
This is a flight data
set on Kaggle website.

00:37:43.370 --> 00:37:45.980
And the good thing is if you
log onto the Kaggle website

00:37:45.980 --> 00:37:48.080
and navigate to any
Kaggle data set,

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:50.930
you'll have a little button
here that says open in,

00:37:50.930 --> 00:37:52.970
and you can click Data Studio.

00:37:52.970 --> 00:37:55.100
You can pick which
file you want.

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:57.710
And that will take you
from the Kaggle interface

00:37:57.710 --> 00:37:59.590
into Data Studio
with the connector

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:00.590
configuration selected.

00:38:00.590 --> 00:38:02.510
And it'll pull in
live data from Kaggle

00:38:02.510 --> 00:38:05.110
and let you do that all the
analysis in Data Studio.

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:07.610
You can also take this data and
blend it with your own data.

00:38:12.340 --> 00:38:14.980
Now, they brought in
the external data.

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:15.630
They merge it.

00:38:15.630 --> 00:38:17.290
Everything has been going well.

00:38:17.290 --> 00:38:19.540
Ruth gets more responsibilities.

00:38:19.540 --> 00:38:21.190
The supply chain
analytics team has

00:38:21.190 --> 00:38:23.320
been merged into Ruth's team.

00:38:23.320 --> 00:38:25.220
They have been doing
a really great job.

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:28.510
But in the supply chain, they
have this prevalent practice

00:38:28.510 --> 00:38:32.047
of using sankey chart for
a lot of their analysis.

00:38:32.047 --> 00:38:33.880
And that's a chart type
that is not natively

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:35.500
available in Data Studio.

00:38:35.500 --> 00:38:37.780
So this is a new problem
that Ruth has to solve.

00:38:37.780 --> 00:38:41.050
I have to make this chart
type available in Data Studio

00:38:41.050 --> 00:38:43.150
because my team needs to use it.

00:38:43.150 --> 00:38:46.135
So how could they do that?

00:38:46.135 --> 00:38:47.510
There is a new
feature available.

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:49.010
It is under developer
preview, which

00:38:49.010 --> 00:38:52.390
is the community visualization.

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:54.880
And here's an example
of a sankey chart

00:38:54.880 --> 00:38:57.300
available in Data Studio.

00:38:57.300 --> 00:39:00.333
What you can do is create your
own chart using JavaScript.

00:39:00.333 --> 00:39:02.250
You can use any popular
library that you want.

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:04.380
You can use D3 or
some other library,

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:07.220
create the chart, user
API, import that chart

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:09.120
into Data Studio,
and then that chart

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:10.650
will get rendered
in Data Studio.

00:39:10.650 --> 00:39:13.863
And it'll work with your
existing data pipeline that

00:39:13.863 --> 00:39:15.030
is available in Data Studio.

00:39:15.030 --> 00:39:16.620
It will work with all
the filters, other chart

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:17.830
types, cross filtering.

00:39:17.830 --> 00:39:20.490
Everything will work
out of the box as it is.

00:39:20.490 --> 00:39:23.280
There is a nice gallery of
community visualizations

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:23.920
available.

00:39:23.920 --> 00:39:26.160
This is made by one
of our partners.

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:27.150
Click inside.

00:39:27.150 --> 00:39:31.590
This is showing small
multiples, which is interactive.

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:34.770
We have scatter chart, which
is another interactive scatter

00:39:34.770 --> 00:39:37.730
chart where you
can do filtering.

00:39:37.730 --> 00:39:38.970
You can exclude this one.

00:39:38.970 --> 00:39:41.137
You can add this back.

00:39:41.137 --> 00:39:42.720
If you want more
information about how

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:45.730
to use community connectors, you
can go into our developer site

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:48.060
and check out the
instructions there.

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:49.980
Now community
visualizations are not

00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:53.280
limited to only the
stuff we have here.

00:39:53.280 --> 00:39:55.800
You can build your own chart,
for example, the one we built

00:39:55.800 --> 00:39:58.990
for the community vis launch.

00:39:58.990 --> 00:40:02.970
This is a chart that shows
the release notes of Data

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:05.050
Studio over the last few years.

00:40:05.050 --> 00:40:08.990
For example, in 2018, these
are all the releases we had.

00:40:08.990 --> 00:40:10.530
And as we hover
over things, you can

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:14.290
see that we launched
this feature in July,

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:16.770
where Data Studio become a
part of the Google Marketing

00:40:16.770 --> 00:40:17.720
platform.

00:40:17.720 --> 00:40:19.740
So if you have a
idea for a chart,

00:40:19.740 --> 00:40:23.340
if you know JavaScript or
have access to a resource that

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:26.820
can build JavaScript charts,
you can bring in your own chart

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:28.860
and render that in Data Studio.

00:40:28.860 --> 00:40:30.420
Now this chart
shows what we have

00:40:30.420 --> 00:40:32.230
done over the last few years.

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:36.300
But to show you what we're going
to do over the next few years,

00:40:36.300 --> 00:40:38.403
here's Riccardo.

00:40:38.403 --> 00:40:40.070
RICCARDO MUTI: All
right, thanks Minhaz.

00:40:40.070 --> 00:40:42.580
So I'll wrap us
up really quickly.

00:40:42.580 --> 00:40:48.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:48.890 --> 00:40:51.120
We want to get to your
questions as well for sure.

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:52.620
But I just want to
talk a little bit

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:54.360
about how we think
about the investments

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:56.040
that we're making
in Data Studio.

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:59.400
Our goal really is to help
you make better decisions

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:01.230
by helping you along
this entire workflow

00:41:01.230 --> 00:41:03.100
from how you connect
to your data,

00:41:03.100 --> 00:41:05.810
how you make it fast with
things like BigQuery BI Engine,

00:41:05.810 --> 00:41:08.310
how you get it into the right
form, how you analyze it using

00:41:08.310 --> 00:41:11.100
the visual tools that we have
like filtering and sorting

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:12.330
and drill down.

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:14.550
Today, I showed
drill down in charts.

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:18.510
Now we're starting to extend
that to pivot tables as well.

00:41:18.510 --> 00:41:21.210
And we want to work on other
types of interactivity that

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:22.830
add analytic value as well.

00:41:22.830 --> 00:41:25.185
On the reporting front,
conditional formatting

00:41:25.185 --> 00:41:27.310
is something that we've
heard from a lot of people.

00:41:27.310 --> 00:41:30.150
And Mary and team are hard
at work on that feature.

00:41:30.150 --> 00:41:32.190
Distribution, we showed
scheduled email today.

00:41:32.190 --> 00:41:34.780
We're interested in other
types of distribution as well.

00:41:34.780 --> 00:41:37.990
And so we have investments
in each one of these areas.

00:41:37.990 --> 00:41:39.720
There's also cross
cutting investments.

00:41:39.720 --> 00:41:42.150
We're always investing in
our developer platform.

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:43.860
We showed community
visualizations,

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:44.880
community connectors.

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:47.050
We're really just getting
started with those.

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:48.717
And so we envision a
world where there's

00:41:48.717 --> 00:41:50.550
lots and lots of
community visualizations

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:53.377
that you can go and choose
from and build upon as well.

00:41:53.377 --> 00:41:55.710
And one of the areas where
we've heard a lot of feedback

00:41:55.710 --> 00:42:00.120
is Data Studio started as this
very, very easy to use tool.

00:42:00.120 --> 00:42:02.670
It looks a lot like Google
Docs and Google Slides.

00:42:02.670 --> 00:42:04.947
And the files get saved
in your Google Drive.

00:42:04.947 --> 00:42:07.030
But what happens when
somebody leaves the company?

00:42:07.030 --> 00:42:09.600
What happens when you want
shared ownership of things?

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:11.420
What if you are a
Google Cloud customer,

00:42:11.420 --> 00:42:13.170
and you manage everything
in GCP projects?

00:42:13.170 --> 00:42:15.295
Wouldn't it be nice if you
could store your reports

00:42:15.295 --> 00:42:18.180
and your data models
in GCP projects?

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:20.100
What if you have Google
Cloud Identity set up

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:21.720
and you have policies
there set up?

00:42:21.720 --> 00:42:23.740
What if you could hook
into those things?

00:42:23.740 --> 00:42:26.250
So one of the things we're
looking at and working on is,

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:29.670
how can we bring more enterprise
administration capabilities

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:32.700
built on Google Cloud
Platform to Data Studio?

00:42:32.700 --> 00:42:34.740
So stay tuned for that.

00:42:34.740 --> 00:42:37.710
Just recapping some of the
things that we showed today,

00:42:37.710 --> 00:42:39.000
these are all available today.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:40.792
They've either been
launched very recently,

00:42:40.792 --> 00:42:42.690
or they have launched
literally this morning.

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:44.490
Drill down is one of them.

00:42:44.490 --> 00:42:48.090
Scheduled email delivery, we
launched earlier this year.

00:42:48.090 --> 00:42:49.560
Sorting launched today as well.

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:51.960
The BigQuery BI Engine,
of course, launched today.

00:42:51.960 --> 00:42:53.668
If you want to stay
on top of this stuff,

00:42:53.668 --> 00:42:55.410
support.Google.com/datastudio.

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:57.502
We publish release
notes every week or so.

00:42:57.502 --> 00:42:59.460
So stay tuned for all
the latest announcements.

